KG42.register("nodes-ab0c8d73487f99d6981d7124",{"id":"COMP-9962fe54ff7e2f44","code":"SET037","label":"Actual-cost compensation based on maximum cost less execution-deviation deductions","definition":"At the end of the period, aggregator compensation is calculated according to ECPM: p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i}c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2. Here, the aggregator itself uses the observed actual cost, while other aggregators use their declared costs evaluated at their respective dispatch instructions.","layer":"L8","template_code":"JOC","concept_id":"settlement.payment","concept_ids":["settlement.payment"],"identity_dimensions":{"identity_fields":["Object: monetary compensation for aggregator flexibility services","Core operation: fixed payment plus actual-cost compensation, deducting a penalty equal to the maximum of mixed costs and a bidirectional quadratic execution-deviation penalty","Output: aggregator compensation amount p_i"]},"exclusions":["The DSO calculates compensation after the time horizon ends based on dispatch instructions, metering results, and payment vouchers.","R_i is a fixed payment intended to support individual rationality and positive revenue.","beta controls the maximum-cost penalty, and gamma controls the execution-deviation penalty.","CPM(13) is retained as the actual-cost compensation and maximum-cost penalty component terms in ECPM(14).","The source's incentive-compatibility condition is \\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}; parameter selection also considers willingness to participate under uncertainty."],"members":[{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction. Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. | DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. | ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty. | Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. | Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. | Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"e2e1bbf6c09250fdda3484fd4e5edbd04edd283e47fe691fc6021e384f76eaa3","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003","node_id":"settlement.payment","function":"settlement.payment","unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","anchor":true}],"paper_ids":["40-fair_dso_flexibility"],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\), in such a way that it makes it a dominant strategy for each aggregator to first make truthful declarations and then follow the flexibility instruction, i.e., \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\approx\\mathbf L_i\\) and \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\approx\\mathbf K_i^*\\), respectively. That is, the reward function \\(p(\\cdot)\\) should be incentive compatible in the sense that it aligns the aggregators’ local objectives with the general objective of the DSO. This needs to be done in order to achieve the optimal solution to problem (11), since this solution guarantees the safe operation of the distribution system and fairness for flexibility assets.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\), in such a way that it makes it a dominant strategy for each aggregator to first make truthful declarations and then follow the flexibility instruction, i.e., \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\approx\\mathbf L_i\\) and \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\approx\\mathbf K_i^*\\), respectively. That is, the reward function \\(p(\\cdot)\\) should be incentive compatible in the sense that it aligns the aggregators’ local objectives with the general objective of the DSO. This needs to be done in order to achieve the optimal solution to problem (11), since this solution guarantees the safe operation of the distribution system and fairness for flexibility assets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN (Remarks and Extensions)","anchor_text":"2254 ECPM provides the DSO with the ability to adjust the devi- ation penalty γ , depending on the s","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"ECPM provides the DSO with the ability to adjust the deviation penalty \\gamma, depending on the system characteristics, so that a good trade-off is achieved between not heavily punishing unintended deviations on one hand and disincentivizing strategic behavior on the other. More generally, the choice of values for parameters \\gamma, R_i and \\beta of ECPM need to be carefully engineered so that the aggregators always benefit from participating in the flexibility market. This means that the penalization for deviations needs to be high enough so as to disincentivize misreporting, but at the same time not so high that aggregators with uncertainties would rather not participate in the market at all, in fear of negative payoffs and heavy penalties.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"ECPM provides the DSO with the ability to adjust the deviation penalty \\gamma, depending on the system characteristics, so that a good trade-off is achieved between not heavily punishing unintended deviations on one hand and disincentivizing strategic behavior on the other. More generally, the choice of values for parameters \\gamma, R_i and \\beta of ECPM need to be carefully engineered so that the aggregators always benefit from participating in the flexibility market. This means that the penalization for deviations needs to be high enough so as to disincentivize misreporting, but at the same time not so high that aggregators with uncertainties would rather not participate in the market at all, in fear of negative payoffs and heavy penalties."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}\\qquad (13)$$","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"$$p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}\\qquad (13)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j and instruction factors \\mathbf{K}_j^* of the rest of the aggregators, j\\neq i, but over the observed cost for i. Parameter \\beta is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j and instruction factors \\mathbf{K}_j^* of the rest of the aggregators, j\\neq i, but over the observed cost for i. Parameter \\beta is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"APPENDIX A","anchor_text":"APPENDIX A PROOF OF LEMMA 1 With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff πi from (12), considerin","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff \\pi_i from (12), considering K_i = K_i^* is given by \\pi_i = -\\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b,j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} \\tag{18}","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff \\pi_i from (12), considering K_i = K_i^* is given by \\pi_i = -\\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b,j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} \\tag{18}"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The payoff of an aggregator \\(i\\), defined as \\(\\pi_i\\), is calculated as the compensation \\(p_i\\) received by the DSO for its flexibility services, minus the actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i\\) paid to its flexible customers according to \\(\\pi_i=p_i-\\tilde c_i\\quad (12)\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The payoff of an aggregator \\(i\\), defined as \\(\\pi_i\\), is calculated as the compensation \\(p_i\\) received by the DSO for its flexibility services, minus the actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i\\) paid to its flexible customers according to \\(\\pi_i=p_i-\\tilde c_i\\quad (12)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions. Regarding the value of \\(\\gamma\\), the following theorem states that the proposed mechanism (14) is incentive compatible for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\).","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions. Regarding the value of \\(\\gamma\\), the following theorem states that the proposed mechanism (14) is incentive compatible for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\), it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters \\((\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\mathbf L_i)\\) and then follow the DSO’s instructions \\((\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i=\\mathbf K_i^*)\\). Proof: The proof is provided in Appendix B.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\), it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters \\((\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\mathbf L_i)\\) and then follow the DSO’s instructions \\((\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i=\\mathbf K_i^*)\\). Proof: The proof is provided in Appendix B."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}, it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters (\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_i=\\mathbf{L}_i) and then follow the DSO’s instructions (\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}}_i=\\mathbf{K}_i^*).\nProof: The proof is provided in Appendix B.","paper":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","quote":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}, it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters (\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_i=\\mathbf{L}_i) and then follow the DSO’s instructions (\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}}_i=\\mathbf{K}_i^*).\nProof: The proof is provided in Appendix B."}],"candidate_only":true,"classification_subtypes":["cost_reimbursement_with_max_cost_and_deviation_penalties"],"projection_statuses":["clear"],"taxonomy":{"primary_family":"kind-L8-5af3572f45","secondary_families":[],"stage":"L8","ontological_kind":"cost_reimbursement_with_max_cost_and_deviation_penalties"},"semantic_definition":{},"source_units":[{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","paper_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","source_occurrences":[{"id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::V::settlement.payment","rule_id":"v141-c-R003","market_meaning":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction. Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM. | DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component. | ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty. | Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation. | Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component. | Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E21"]},"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010"],"decision_anchor":"settlement.payment","source_rule_sha256":"e2e1bbf6c09250fdda3484fd4e5edbd04edd283e47fe691fc6021e384f76eaa3"}],"constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The optimal local flexibility-allocation instructions feed compensation calculation: other aggregators' declared costs are evaluated at their instructions for the maximum-cost penalty, and the recipient's implemented flexibility is compared with its instruction for the deviation penalty. Measured consumption and proof of payments also enter the calculation. Scope: Post-horizon ECPM compensation in the DSO flexibility market, including its inherited CPM component.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E15","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Let \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\{\\hat\\lambda_{i,t},\\hat{\\overline K}_{i,t},\\hat{\\underline K}_{i,t}\\}\\) denote the aggregator’s declaration for \\(\\mathbf L_i\\) which may or may not be equal to the actual \\(\\mathbf L_i\\). The DSO solves (11) using the declarations \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as input, and the optimal solution is given to each aggregator as an instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to follow."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The DSO designs the aggregator compensation function and chooses its penalization factor beta. Scope: DSO design of the proposed aggregator compensation rule and its inherited CPM component.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E18","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C002","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The task of the DSO in this case is to design the reward function, \\(p_i\\), in such a way that it makes it a dominant strategy for each aggregator to first make truthful declarations and then follow the flexibility instruction, i.e., \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\approx\\mathbf L_i\\) and \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\approx\\mathbf K_i^*\\), respectively. That is, the reward function \\(p(\\cdot)\\) should be incentive compatible in the sense that it aligns the aggregators’ local objectives with the general objective of the DSO. This needs to be done in order to achieve the optimal solution to problem (11), since this solution guarantees the safe operation of the distribution system and fairness for flexibility assets."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"ECPM includes a fixed payment to each aggregator intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff. Scope: Fixed-payment component of ECPM compensation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C004","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"ECPM compensation includes reimbursement of the aggregator's actual flexibility cost as a component, alongside its penalty deductions. Scope: Actual-cost reimbursement within the CPM component inherited by ECPM.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E24"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C004","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C005","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The deviation penalty is quadratic in deviation volume and applies in both upward and downward directions. Its per-unit penalization increases with deviation magnitude, strongly penalizing large deviations while keeping small, possibly unintended deviations mild. Scope: Shape of the ECPM monetary penalty for deviations from the DSO instruction. Condition: An aggregator implements flexibility that deviates from the DSO instruction.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C005","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C006","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Deviation penalties must be high enough to deter misreporting while avoiding levels that make aggregators with uncertainty prefer to opt out because of potential negative payoffs and heavy penalties. Scope: ECPM deviation-penalty selection, including participation by aggregators with uncertainty.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-E21"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p06-c1r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN (Remarks and Extensions)","anchor_text":"2254 ECPM provides the DSO with the ability to adjust the devi- ation penalty γ , depending on the s","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"ECPM provides the DSO with the ability to adjust the deviation penalty \\gamma, depending on the system characteristics, so that a good trade-off is achieved between not heavily punishing unintended deviations on one hand and disincentivizing strategic behavior on the other. More generally, the choice of values for parameters \\gamma, R_i and \\beta of ECPM need to be carefully engineered so that the aggregators always benefit from participating in the flexibility market. This means that the penalization for deviations needs to be high enough so as to disincentivize misreporting, but at the same time not so high that aggregators with uncertainties would rather not participate in the market at all, in fear of negative payoffs and heavy penalties."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"ECPM payment rule (14): the payment to aggregator i equals the fixed payment R_i plus the observed actual cost, minus beta times the maximum of the observed actual cost and the worst declared cost among other aggregators evaluated at their instructions, minus a quadratic deviation penalty summed over the horizon.","expression":"p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L}) = R_i + \\tilde c_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\right\\} - \\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","coordination.local_operator","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_beta","var_gamma","var_R_i","var_tildeK_i"],"parameter_refs":["par_K_i_star","par_tilde_c_i","par_hatL_j","par_c_j_eval","par_Omega_b","par_Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M01-activation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"that is not carefully designed, might still result in strategic misreporting. Let Li = { λi,t, Ki,t,","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Once the instruction is communicated to each aggregator for the horizon \\(\\Omega_T\\), real-time operation begins. The aggregator implements a flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), where the tilde denotes the fact that this is the actual final flexibility factor implemented by the aggregator, as measured by the DSO’s smart meters. In real time operation, two things may go differently than scheduled: • The aggregator might not follow the instruction, and implement a different final profile, i.e., \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne \\mathbf K_i^*\\). • The actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i,\\mathbf L_i)\\) that the aggregator pays to its customers for the implementation of the flexibility profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\), may not be equal to the cost calculated by the DSO, \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\), e.g., aggregators providing false declarations of flexibility costs. Note that both cases result in inefficiency, since even after assuming that the aggregator perfectly follows the instruction, the costs can still differ, i.e., \\(\\tilde c_i = c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\mathbf L_i)\\ne c_i(\\mathbf K_i^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M01-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and mes- 1) sage exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First,","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"A general overview of the proposed procedure and message exchange is depicted in Fig. 1. First, the DSO is informed about the aggregators’ expected demand profiles \\(\\mathbf P^D_i\\) for a given time horizon ahead. The DSO checks whether the network constraints can be satisfied. In case they cannot be satisfied, the DSO calls for the aggregators’ flexibility bids. The aggregators make their flexibility bids \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) to the DSO. Given the bids, the DSO solves (11) and communicates the instruction \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\) to each aggregator \\(i\\). Given the instruction, the aggregator manages its portfolio at real time operation. The aggregator’s actual load profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\) can be measured by the DSO. After the end of the horizon, the aggregator compensates its flexible agents and communicates the “proof of payment” to the DSO, i.e., the voucher \\(\\tilde c_i\\). This could also be done automatically, e.g., via a smart contract. Given the aggregator’s instruction and observation (i.e., measured consumption and proof of payments), the DSO calculates the aggregator’s compensation \\(p_i\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Inherited CPM payment rule (13): the CPM reward equals observed actual cost minus beta times the maximum of the observed actual cost and the maximum declared cost of other aggregators evaluated at their instructions.","expression":"p_i^{CPM} = \\tilde c_i - \\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\right\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["clearing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_beta"],"parameter_refs":["par_tilde_c_i","par_K_i_star","par_hatL_j","par_c_j_eval","par_Omega_b"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-E11","v141-b-g02-E12","v141-b-g03-E07"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For the design of the reward function \\(p_i\\) we consider the family of mechanisms referred to as compensation and penalty mechanisms (CPM), as in [36, Sec. 10.6.1]. In our context, the aggregators’ instructions \\(\\mathbf K_i^*\\), are calculated by solving problem (11), but using the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i\\) as the input that defines the functions \\(c_i(\\mathbf K_i,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i)\\). After the observation of the outcome, the aggregator’s reward is calculated by \\(p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}\\quad (13)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"calculated at the end of the horizon based on the measurements of the actual power injections. The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\(\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j\\) and instruction factors \\(\\mathbf K_j^*\\) of the rest of the aggregators, \\(j\\ne i\\), but over the observed cost for \\(i\\). Parameter \\(\\beta\\) is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E11","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"gic IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN on For the design of the reward function pi we consider the he family","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i^{CPM}=\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}\\qquad (13)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E12","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"Fig. 1. Procedure and message exchange between DSO and Aggregator. calculated at the end of the hori","equation_id":"(13)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term in (13) compensates the aggregator for its actual flexibility cost that the latter offered at its flexible assets. The second term is the worst-off flexibility cost among aggregators, where the cost is calculated over the declared tuples \\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j and instruction factors \\mathbf{K}_j^* of the rest of the aggregators, j\\neq i, but over the observed cost for i. Parameter \\beta is a penalization factor chosen by the DSO."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E07","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p09-c1r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":9,"printed_page":null,"section":"APPENDIX A","anchor_text":"APPENDIX A PROOF OF LEMMA 1 With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff πi from (12), considerin","equation_id":"(18)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"With payment rule (13), the aggregator’s payoff \\pi_i from (12), considering K_i = K_i^* is given by \\pi_i = -\\beta \\max\\left\\{\\tilde c_i, \\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b,j\\neq i}\\left\\{c_j(K_j^*,\\hat L_j)\\right\\}\\right\\} \\tag{18}"}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The ECPM deviation-penalty component is the negative sum over the horizon of gamma times the squared difference between the actual implemented flexibility factor and the DSO instruction factor.","expression":"-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_gamma","var_tildeK_i"],"parameter_refs":["par_K_i_star","par_Omega_T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E17","v141-b-g02-E16","v141-b-g02-E17"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, in practice, aggregators are not hard-constrained to follow the instruction. In fact, the aggregator can instead opt for a profile \\(\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i\\ne\\mathbf K_i^*\\). In this case, the original CPM mechanism is not enough to guarantee incentive compatibility. We propose a novel payment rule, called enhanced-CPM (ECPM), modifying the CPM rule as stated by \\(p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i} c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2\\quad (14)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"re- The ﬁrst term, Ri, is a ﬁxed payment to each aggregator, in 3) order to make sure that the mecha","equation_id":"(13),(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The first term, \\(R_i\\), is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\(\\gamma\\), that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p04-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. PROBLEM FORMULATION","anchor_text":"re The payoff of an aggregator i, deﬁned as πi, is calculated te, as the compensation pi received by","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The payoff of an aggregator \\(i\\), defined as \\(\\pi_i\\), is calculated as the compensation \\(p_i\\) received by the DSO for its flexibility services, minus the actual cost \\(\\tilde c_i\\) paid to its flexible customers according to \\(\\pi_i=p_i-\\tilde c_i\\quad (12)\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E16","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"g machine has no option but to execute its tasks, and this is as why the assumption that the aggrega","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"$$p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}},\\hat{\\mathbf{L}})=R_i+\\tilde{c}_i-\\beta\\max\\left\\{\\tilde{c}_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\neq i}c_j\\left(\\mathbf{K}_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_j\\right)\\right\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_{\\mathbb{T}}}\\gamma\\left(\\tilde{K}_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*\\right)^2\\qquad (14)$$"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E17","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The first term, R_i, is a fixed payment to each aggregator, in order to make sure that the mechanism is individually rational, i.e., the aggregators’ payoff is always positive which makes the aggregators eager to participate in the mechanism rather than opt out. The middle term is (13), while the last term is a penalty controlled by parameter \\gamma, that has been introduced in order to penalize the aggregator’s deviation from the DSO’s instruction. The penalty imposed to the deviating aggregator is quadratic in the volume of deviation, so as to penalize deviations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit penalization is increasing in the amount of deviation, which helps towards penalizing large deviations strongly, but keeping the penalty of small (possibly unintended) deviations mild. The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions."}]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R003-C010","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The source states the incentive-compatibility condition on the deviation-penalty coefficient as gamma much greater than the aggregator's flexibility cost parameter lambda_i,t.","expression":"\\gamma \\gg \\lambda_{i,t}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":["var_gamma"],"parameter_refs":["par_lambda_i_t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E25","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-E18"],"ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010","source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E25","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The last term is not included in the original CPM mechanism described in [36], but it is necessary in order to incentivize the aggregators to follow the instructions. Regarding the value of \\(\\gamma\\), the following theorem states that the proposed mechanism (14) is incentive compatible for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\(\\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}\\), it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters \\((\\hat{\\mathbf L}_i=\\mathbf L_i)\\) and then follow the DSO’s instructions \\((\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i=\\mathbf K_i^*)\\). Proof: The proof is provided in Appendix B."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-E18","location":{"location_id":"src-7a81b1c717-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"IV. REWARD FUNCTION DESIGN","anchor_text":"eir ations in both directions (up and down). Also, the per-unit is penalization is increasing in the","equation_id":"(14)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Theorem 1: Given payment structure (14), for \\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}, it is a dominant strategy for each aggregator to declare its true parameters (\\hat{\\mathbf{L}}_i=\\mathbf{L}_i) and then follow the DSO’s instructions (\\tilde{\\mathbf{K}}_i=\\mathbf{K}_i^*).\nProof: The proof is provided in Appendix B."}]}],"variables":[{"variable_id":"var_beta","symbol":"\\beta","meaning":"Penalization factor in the compensation-and-penalty payment rule, chosen by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar penalization factor","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-E21","v141-b-g02-E16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_beta"},{"variable_id":"var_gamma","symbol":"\\gamma","meaning":"Per-unit coefficient of the quadratic deviation penalty, adjustable by the DSO.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; DSO-chosen scalar satisfying the stated incentive and participation trade-off","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E25","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_gamma"},{"variable_id":"var_R_i","symbol":"R_i","meaning":"Fixed payment to aggregator i in ECPM, intended to ensure individual rationality and positive payoff.","kind":"design_selection","domain":"source does not state a numeric domain; fixed payment amount for aggregator i","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-E17","v141-b-g02-E21"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_R_i"},{"variable_id":"var_tildeK_i","symbol":"\\tilde{\\mathbf K}_i","meaning":"Actual final flexibility profile implemented by aggregator i and measured by the DSO, possibly differing from the instruction.","kind":"operational","domain":"profile over t in \\Omega_T of measured actual flexibility factors; source does not state explicit numerical bounds for the measured profile","unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E16"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::x::var_tildeK_i"}],"parameters":[{"parameter_id":"par_K_i_star","symbol":"\\mathbf K_i^*","meaning":"Optimal flexibility instruction communicated to aggregator i from the clearing problem using the declared tuples.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"clearing instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_K_i_star"},{"parameter_id":"par_tilde_c_i","symbol":"\\tilde c_i","meaning":"Observed actual flexibility cost of aggregator i after the horizon, used as the actual-cost reimbursement term and in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"observed actual cost"},"unit":null,"indices":["i"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E16","v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g03-E15"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_tilde_c_i"},{"parameter_id":"par_hatL_j","symbol":"\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j","meaning":"Declared parameter tuple of another aggregator j, used in the maximum-cost penalty term.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared parameter tuple of aggregator j"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E15","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E06"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_hatL_j"},{"parameter_id":"par_c_j_eval","symbol":"c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)","meaning":"Declared flexibility cost of another aggregator j evaluated at its instruction and declared tuple in the maximum-cost penalty.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"declared cost evaluated at instruction"},"unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-E11"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_c_j_eval"},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_b","symbol":"\\Omega_b","meaning":"Set of nodes/aggregators over which the maximum-cost penalty and sums are indexed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of nodes/aggregators"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_Omega_b"},{"parameter_id":"par_Omega_T","symbol":"\\Omega_T","meaning":"Set of time periods over which the quadratic deviation penalty is summed.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"set of time periods"},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E31"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_Omega_T"},{"parameter_id":"par_lambda_i_t","symbol":"\\lambda_{i,t}","meaning":"Flexibility cost parameter of aggregator i at period t, entering the cost function and the gamma-dominant condition.","value":{"kind":"symbolic","number_value":null,"text_value":"flexibility cost parameter"},"unit":"$/kWh^2","indices":["i","t"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E32","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g01-E10"],"source_rule_id":"v141-c-R003","declaration_ref":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::theta::par_lambda_i_t"}]}],"source_definitions":[{"unit_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","stage":"L8","subtype":"cost_reimbursement_with_max_cost_and_deviation_penalties","projection_status":"clear","target_definition":"At the end of the period, aggregator compensation is calculated according to ECPM: p_i(\\tilde{\\mathbf K},\\hat{\\mathbf L})=R_i+\\tilde c_i-\\beta\\max\\{\\tilde c_i,\\max_{j\\in\\Omega_b:j\\ne i}c_j(\\mathbf K_j^*,\\hat{\\mathbf L}_j)\\}-\\sum_{t\\in\\Omega_T}\\gamma(\\tilde K_{i,t}-K_{i,t}^*)^2. Here, the aggregator itself uses the observed actual cost, while other aggregators use their declared costs evaluated at their respective dispatch instructions.","form_kind":"mathematical","identity_fields":["Object: monetary compensation for aggregator flexibility services","Core operation: fixed payment plus actual-cost compensation, deducting a penalty equal to the maximum of mixed costs and a bidirectional quadratic execution-deviation penalty","Output: aggregator compensation amount p_i"],"variation_fields":["The DSO calculates compensation after the time horizon ends based on dispatch instructions, metering results, and payment vouchers.","R_i is a fixed payment intended to support individual rationality and positive revenue.","beta controls the maximum-cost penalty, and gamma controls the execution-deviation penalty.","CPM(13) is retained as the actual-cost compensation and maximum-cost penalty component terms in ECPM(14).","The source's incentive-compatibility condition is \\gamma\\gg\\lambda_{i,t}; parameter selection also considers willingness to participate under uncertainty."],"constraint_refs":["40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C001","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C003","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C006","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C007","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C008","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C009","40-fair_dso_flexibility::v141-c-R003::v141-c-R003-C010"],"paper_id":"40-fair_dso_flexibility","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","function":"settlement.payment","template_code":"JOC"}],"source_variants":[],"functional":{"is_child":false,"subdivision_pending":false},"canonical_pointer":"/nodes/188"});
